A||||23||fieroshop||Update on Borla||||10
Z||000000||fieroshop||11-02-1999||09:50 AM||||It seems that the exhaust system that is going to be built for me is a true copy of the Borla exhaust and the muffler is a Borla muffler. We are also contemplating on building stainless steel headers for this system. So this system will be all stainless from the heads to the exhuast tips and instead of pipe sliding into each other all parts will have a flange so that all you would have to do is bolt everything together. No price at this time.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Eric||11-02-1999||12:35 PM||||If it's less than $1000 it would be worthwhile.  Now this kit includes everything from the headers to the Y pipe to the cat to the exhaust to the muffler to the tips, right?  If the price is right, you've got me sold and a lot of other Fiero owners.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-02-1999||12:40 PM||||I'd say sometime in the next year or two, I'm going to have to replace my muffler(can't believe GM skimped on the exhaust, my '88 Sable has a complete stainless system).  Prices would be great, parts and complete system.  I don't like doing things twice. "Buy the best, buy it once.  Do it right, do it once."--My motto.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric||11-02-1999||12:49 PM||||The Fiero exhausts are all stainless.  They should last forever, but they are a little restrictive.  The main reason most of us want a new exhaust is to gain power.  I'm guessing this system should be good for 15-20 hp if it's anything like some of the others I've seen. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||11-02-1999||12:53 PM||||Fieroshop, are you planning on coating the headers with Jet Hot?  It may be a good selling point if the Y pipe was coated too.  That would seriously cut down on heat in the engine compartment and give a few more ponies as a plus.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||11-02-1999||08:12 PM||||I'm not just talking the pipes, I mean I can push on my muffler it will flex pretty good. I bet if I push hard enough with my finger it would go through. It's also pretty rusty and ratty looking.  No leaks, and it's not too loud, but it's nothing like the one on that sable.  The thing has almost 200 thousand miles on it, and that entire exhaust system is showroom clean.  The Fiero only has 155 on it and looks like crap.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-02-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DXR_DAD||11-02-1999||10:08 PM||||Fieroshop if all the parts are flanged, will you sell just the muffler alone?   ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||11-03-1999||01:41 AM||||I don't think that the Fiero really has that restrictive of an exhaust, there just isn't enough of it.  Compare it to a normal car where it has to run from the front to the back...  The V6 headers could use a little work, tho.  just mho||||reg||5
Z||000008||lowCG||11-03-1999||02:49 AM||||It depends,but usually 12-14" of collector is required for proper extraction effect,after that it's just a matter of style and preference.I would like to see some headers with larger primary tubes,slightly larger collector,that seems to work well I've found.Maybe 1 5/8",or 1 3/4",and 2 1/2".<BR>The small stock system allows the engine to wind up smoothly,but more mid range torque could easily be had with some changes.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieroshop||11-03-1999||08:58 AM||||I'll probably sell the components individually and or together. Still don't know what price we are going to have but hopefully I can keep the cost down to a modest roar and a hurricane.||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||11-05-1999||11:19 PM||||Before you call the Fiero system restrictive, remove the O2 and screw in a pressure gauge (0-10 PSI) and go for a drive. You will be surprised. At WOT, a stock systen rarely exceeds 2 PSI.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||11-06-1999||04:04 PM||||that would confirm allot of what I'd thought.... as long as the cat and mufler are good, then the stock exhust isn't a problem.<P>The main problem to good scavaging is the cruddy manifolds, not the plumbing from there back.||||reg||1
Z||000012||DXR_DAD||11-06-1999||04:39 PM||||Fieroshop we would like to hear your thoughts  and input on what terryk and ogre say about this, what have you seen in your shop? What other exhaust mods are you doing? ||||reg||5
Z||000013||fieroshop||11-06-1999||09:03 PM||||Well it depends where you measure the pressure. Beleive it or not the pressure is much higher then 2 lbs and that is not the problem anuway. The problem is the restriction that the stock system has. That means the manifolds, th estock cat and the exhaust system all have a restriction and that is a loss of horsepower. By removing some of the restriction then you are able to get better gas mileage, better performance and added horesepower. If you don't have enough restriction or too much restriction as is the case of stock systems, then you get the reverse.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Bruce||11-06-1999||10:56 PM||||Where do you think the best places (eg: manifold, cat, muffler, tubing) are to remove restriction? What steps take you so far that you don't have enough back pressure for good low-end torque?||||reg||5
Z||000015||fieroshop||11-06-1999||11:50 PM||||Well it definitly is not to have any pressure in the manifolds. That is the reason that the Fiero V6 gets manifolds that crack. Especially the left one or to those that don't know what is the left it would be the one closest to the firewall. The restriction has to come from the rest of the system and it is better if it was somewhat even.||||reg||7
Z||000016||Bruce||11-07-1999||12:08 AM||||My plan was (is) to have the manifolds ported, install a catco converter, and replace the stock muffler with the flowmaster series 80. At this point, I think I'll keep the tubing size at the stock dimension. Does this get me adequate or too little back pressure?||||reg||5
Z||000017||DXR_DAD||11-07-1999||12:40 AM||||Bruce, Whats up?  That is a better move than larger tubing. Remember you don't want 0 back presure and you don't want it to be too LOUD with that flowmaster. So be like Nike  "Just do it" <P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Bruce||11-07-1999||12:19 PM||||Bill, <BR>the flowmaster is still in the box- I'm a little hesitant installing it because of the comments that it might be too loud. Maybe I should send it back and just do the porting, low restriction cat thing. Would like to hear a little more on the flowmaster from others who have used it.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Old Lar||11-07-1999||05:19 PM||||Fiero Parts and Appraisals (FOCONE) had Borla systems available.  I bought two, one for my 88 GT now installed and one for my 87 GT. I'm waiting for the exhaust to go on the 87.<P>The 88 had a new cat put on when the exhaust went on.  The 88 sounds good with the Borla system. ||||reg||2
Z||000020||Bruce||11-07-1999||06:18 PM||||What to do, what to do with the Flowmaster???||||reg||5
Z||000021||fieroshop||11-07-1999||09:58 PM||||Bruce, If you have a stock system then what I would do is port out the exhaust manifolds, install a stainless steel high flow cat and go ahead and install the flowmaster. <BR>I believe that you are from the SF Bay Area and if you are having problems doing  something about your system give me a call.<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 11-07-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000022||cmyokley||11-08-1999||06:11 PM||||Bruce,<P>I put a custom exhaust system in my 86gt using a delta series flowmaster and it sounds great. It is louder than the OEM but sounds much better. It greatly reduced the back pressure in the system.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000023||Bruce||11-08-1999||09:34 PM||||Then, Flowmaster it is!<BR>Thanks, FS and CW.||||reg||4
